[=#oracleforeignbackend]
= Foreign Backend =

Since SQL Relay supports a variety of database back-ends, the app can also be redirected to any of these databases, instead of the Oracle database it was originally written to use.

This is as simple as specifying a different database using the //dbase// attribute in the instance tag and specifying the appropriate database connection string in the //string// attribute of the connection tag.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-oraclefemysql.conf@
}}}
}}}

In this example, an Oracle front-end provides Oracle applications access to a !MySQL back-end database.

Depending on the back-end, it might also be necessary to make some other configuration changes, as well, for example:

* In the instance tag:
 * set translatebindvariables="yes"
 * set the isolationlevel attribute
 * set transactionmodel="implicit" - the transaction model that an app written for Oracle would expect
* In the string attribute of the connection tag:
 * set autocommit=yes or autocommit=no
 * set the identity parameter to override the database type
  * setting identity=oracle causes integer columns and numeric columns without declared scale to be reported as NUMBER(38)
 * set ignorespace=yes
 * set the overrideschema parameter
 * set the typemangling and/or tablemangling parameters - when using a !PostgreSQL back-end
* use [querytranslation.html#querytranslation Query Translation] modules
* use [resultsettranslation.html#resultsettranslation Result Set Translation] modules
* use [errortranslation.html#errortranslation Error Translation] modules
* use [sessionqueries.html#sessionqueries Session Queries]

If [querytranslation.html#querytranslation Query Translations] aren't sufficient, then it might still be necessary to modify some queries to use the syntax of the foreign database, or even update some code, but a full rewrite of the app should not be necessary.

